<PageLayout
  title="MC3R — the energy-partitioning receptor"
  kicker="Receptor · Melanocortin 3 receptor"
  description="MC4R's quieter sibling in the hypothalamus. Rather than switching appetite on and off, MC3R governs how the body spends its energy — and, it turns out, the timing of growth and puberty."
  thread="The system's quiet accountant — not whether the body eats, but how it spends: the same melanocortin signal tuned to balance rather than command."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Melanocortin 3 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_3_receptor'] },
  ]}
>
  <ReceptorFacts receptor={receptor} />

  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'MC3R is MC4R’s hypothalamic sibling: rather than switching appetite on and off, it governs how the body partitions and spends energy.',
      'It also helps set the timing of growth and puberty, and contributes to sodium handling (natriuresis).',
      'MC3R is required to keep time with meals — to anticipate feeding and synchronise metabolism to when food arrives.',
      'Two common variants (Thr6Lys + Val81Ile), inherited together, partially blunt the receptor and track with pediatric-onset obesity.',
      'It is activated by all the MSH peptides and ACTH, and antagonised by AgRP.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    MC3R shares the hypothalamus with MC4R and responds to the same melanocortin
    peptides, yet it does a different job. For years it was the harder of the two
    central receptors to pin down — clearly involved in energy balance, but not in
    the simple "eat / don't eat" way of MC4R.<Cite n={1} /> Recent human genetics has
    given it a much sharper identity.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="The same melanocortin signal splits between two receptors. MC4R sets how much energy the body acquires; MC3R governs how that energy is spent — partitioning, the tempo of growth and puberty, and the timing of meals."
    source="Schematic, after Cone 2006 and Lam et al. 2021."
  >
    <OneSignalTwoJobs />
  </Figure>

  <h2>Energy partitioning, not just intake</h2>
  <p>
    Like the rest of the family, MC3R couples to Gs and cyclic AMP, and γ-MSH is a
    relatively potent agonist at this subtype while AgRP antagonises it.<Cite n={3} />
    But the phenotype of losing MC3R is distinctive: animal models point less to
    raw appetite and more to how energy is <em>partitioned</em> — the balance of
    fat versus lean mass and the efficiency with which food is used. MC3R also acts
    within the hypothalamic circuitry itself, modulating the POMC and AgRP neurons
    that feed into MC4R.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>The growth-and-puberty switch</h2>
  <p>
    The clearest picture of MC3R's purpose came in 2021. People carrying
    loss-of-function MC3R variants — including a rare individual with two defective
    copies — were found to have later puberty, reduced linear growth, lower lean
    mass, and lower circulating IGF-1; mice lacking Mc3r showed delayed sexual
    maturation and a reproductive cycle that no longer responded normally to
    nutritional state.<Cite n={2} /> The interpretation is elegant: the same
    nutrient-sensing melanocortin system bifurcates — signalling through MC4R to
    control how much energy is acquired, and through MC3R to decide how that energy
    is <em>spent</em> on growing up.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>The MC3R gene and its common double variant</h2>
  <p>
    MC3R is a compact, single-coding-exon gene on chromosome 20 (20q13.2), and
    like the other receptors its function can be read straight from its variants.
    Two common coding changes — <strong>Thr6Lys</strong> and <strong>Val81Ile</strong> —
    sit in near-complete linkage disequilibrium, so they are almost always
    inherited together as a single haplotype. In the lab that double variant is
    <em>partially</em> inactivating: fewer receptors reach the cell surface and
    each signals less strongly than wild-type.<Cite n={4} /> In children, carrying
    both copies tracks with higher body weight, more fat mass, greater leptin and
    insulin, and reduced fat oxidation — a subtler contribution to common obesity
    than the outright switch-failure seen with MC4R.<Cite n={4} /> The rarer,
    severe loss-of-function alleles are the ones that surface as delayed growth and
    puberty rather than obesity alone.<Cite n={2} /> Surface delivery is itself
    tunable: the accessory protein <strong>MRAP2</strong> modulates how much MC3R
    reaches the membrane and how it responds.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>Keeping time with meals</h2>
  <p>
    MC3R has one job that is entirely its own: it helps the body <em>anticipate</em>
    food. When animals are fed only at a fixed time, they normally ramp up activity
    and wakefulness in the hours beforehand and shift their metabolism to meet the
    meal — food-anticipatory behaviour. Mice lacking MC3R lose that anticipation
    and no longer synchronise their peripheral clocks and fuel use to feeding
    time.<Cite n={5} /> It reframes the receptor as part of the link between
    <em>when</em> the body is fed and how its internal clocks are set — nutritional
    timing, not just nutritional quantity, and a role no other melanocortin
    receptor covers.<Cite n={5} />
  </p>

  <h2>Inflammation and the periphery</h2>
  <p>
    MC3R is not confined to the brain. It is expressed in the gut, heart, kidney,
    placenta, and on immune cells, where melanocortin signalling through MC3R is
    broadly anti-inflammatory and pro-resolving — part of why the receptor is
    studied as a target in inflammatory disease.<Cite n={1} /> For how MC3R sits
    alongside the other four receptors, see the{' '}
    <a href="/system">overview pillar</a>; for the appetite arm it complements, see{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A receptor coming into focus</h2>
  <p>
    For most of its history MC3R was defined by what it was <em>not</em> — not the
    pigment receptor, not the cortisol receptor, not quite the appetite switch that
    MC4R is. The 2021 growth-and-puberty work changed that, giving it a role no
    other receptor covers: translating nutritional state into the tempo of
    development.<Cite n={2} /> That has also made MC3R a target of fresh
    pharmacological interest. Because it sits within the same hypothalamic circuitry
    as MC4R and modulates those neurons, selective MC3R agonists are being explored
    as a way to fine-tune the appetite system alongside MC4R drugs — part of the
    wider <a href="/therapeutics">therapeutics</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a>.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

